Hi,

Thank you for your clear answer, I better understand now, my "bird" recording is composed of only one species of bird, so it's not so high I guess.

I have another question concerning this index. If I understand correctly what I read, the "Acoustic Richness Index" is similar to the H index, but more suitable to the temperate climates (according to the article Monitoring animal diversity using acoustic indices: Implementation in a tempate woodland). However, when I try to compute it for an audio file in R, I can see this suggestion in the console:
Warning message:
In AR(birds) :
  Computing the AR index on a single object does not make a lot of sense as AR is a ranked index

I don't really understand why it is needed to compute that index with several objects. For me the AR is the same than the H index but with a slight correction in order to avoid the effect of the background noise and the low amplitude of the acoustic activity. I tried with several audio files and the AR index change according the number of objects that I use to compute the index. Can someone can explain me the difference?

Thank you!

Jeremy

